A '''''Star Wars''''' film focusing on [[Boba Fett]] was planned to be directed by [[Josh Trank]] from a screenplay by [[Simon Kinberg]]. Reports suggested that the film was to center on [[bounty hunter]] [[Boba Fett]] and was slated for a release in [[2018]]. Trank left the project in [[2015]], however, following troubles on the set, poor reception of his film [[Wikipedia:Fantastic Four (2015 film)|''Fantastic Four'']], and Lucasfilm's reluctance to continue with him as director. The film was originally going to be the second installment of the ''[[Star Wars Anthology Series]]'' spin-off films.

Following the release of ''[[Solo: A Star Wars Story]]'', Disney later announced that a third stand-alone picture was planned for [[2020]], but neither the Boba Fett focus nor Kinberg's continued involvement were confirmed. However, in May [[2019]], Disney released its theatrical schedule up through 2027, and no ''Star Wars'' film was listed until [[2022]]. This schedule was further changed as a result of the COVID-19 pandemic, with the next film being slated for [[2023]]. Kinberg confirmed in 2023 that his project was centered around Boba Fett and was dropped from development.

==Development==
{{Quote|At the point I was doing it I was probably scaring the shit out of everyone. I was making much more of a borderline R-rated, single planet spaghetti Western. They probably would never be able to embrace Baby Yoda if I had made that. It didn't really belong in the world I was kind of envisioning.|[[James Mangold]]|<ref name="Variety Canceled">{{WebCite|url=https://variety.com/2023/film/news/boba-fett-movie-r-rated-canceled-solo-box-office-1235658248|text=Axed Boba Fett Movie Was a 'Borderline R-Rated' Spaghetti Western That 'Probably Scared the S— Out of Everyone,' Says James Mangold|date=2023-06-29|author=Zack Sharf|work=[[Wikipedia:Variety (magazine)|''Variety'']]|archivedate=20230630005505}}</ref>}}
In [[2012]], [[The Walt Disney Company]] acquired [[Lucasfilm]] and began planning the future of the ''[[Star Wars]]'' franchise. They immediately announced their intention to produce the [[sequel trilogy]],<ref name="Acquisition">{{SW|url=news/new-star-wars-movies-announced-as-disney-enters-agreement-to-acquire-lucasfilm-ltd|text=New Star Wars Movies Announced as Disney Enters Agreement to Acquire Lucasfilm Ltd.}}</ref> and that announcement was soon followed by news of several [[Star Wars Anthology Series|stand-alone pictures]] that were not connected to the core [[Skywalker family]] saga. This was confirmed by [[Bob Iger (chief executive officer)|Bob Iger]], the chairman and CEO of The Walt Disney Company, on February 5, [[2013]]. Iger further stated that there would be at least two stand-alone films, one written by [[Lawrence Kasdan]] and another by [[Simon Kinberg]].<ref name="CNBC SW Spinoffs" /> The first stand-alone ultimately became [[2016]]'s ''[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]'',<ref name="First Stand-Alone">{{SW|url=news/rogue-one-is-the-first-star-wars-stand-alone-film-rian-johnson-to-write-and-direct-star-wars-episode-viii|text=''Rogue One'' Is the First ''Star Wars'' Stand-Alone Film, Rian Johnson to Write and Direct ''Star Wars'': Episode VIII}}</ref> while Kasdan wrote [[2018]]'s ''[[Solo: A Star Wars Story]]'' with his son [[Jon Kasdan|Jon]].<ref name="Han Solo">{{SW|url=news/christopher-miller-and-phil-lord-to-helm-han-solo-anthology-film|text=Christopher Miller and Phil Lord to Helm Han Solo Anthology Film|nolive=1}}</ref>

[[File:Boba_Fett_HS_Fathead.png|thumb|left|200px|Rumors had suggested that this film would focus on Boba Fett.]]
On [[June 14]], [[2014]], it was announced that Kinberg's film was to be directed by [[Josh Trank]].<ref name="Trank Announced" /> Reports have suggested that the film would center on the [[bounty hunter]] [[Boba Fett]].<ref name="EW Aug-2015">Breznican, Anthony. "Fantastic Flameout." [[Entertainment Weekly Books|''Entertainment Weekly'']] 21 Aug. 2015: 16-17. Print. "In the months that followed, Trank continued pushing for his edit and participated in a PR campaign to minimize rumors of discord on set. Few bought it, including Lucasfilm president Kathleen Kennedy, who had hired him in June 2014 to direct a ''Star Wars'' stand-alone film about Boba Fett for 2018."</ref> According to [[Entertainment Weekly Books|''Entertainment Weekly'']], the story of Trank's film would have focused not only on Fett but also on the menagerie of bounty hunters seen along Fett on [[Star Wars: Episode V The Empire Strikes Back|''Star Wars'': Episode V ''The Empire Strikes Back'']]&ndash;[[4-LOM]], [[Bossk'wassak'Cradossk|Bossk]], [[Dengar]], [[IG-88B|IG-88]], and [[Zuckuss]].<ref name="Trank's Story">{{WebCite|url=https://ew.com/movies/2018/10/26/star-wars-producers-halt-boba-fett-film|text=''Star Wars'' producers halt unannounced Boba Fett standalone film|author=Breznican, Anthony|date=2018-10-26|work=[[Entertainment Weekly Books|''Entertainment Weekly'']]|archivedate=20200108015616}}</ref> [[Daniel Keys Moran]], author of the famous [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wars'' Legends]] short story "[[The Last One Standing: The Tale of Boba Fett]]," mentioned in an interview that a Disney employee had been emailing him about the possibility of Lucasfilm adapting his short story into the plot of a Boba Fett-centric film.<ref name="Moran interview">{{WebCite|url=https://starwarsinterviews.com/various/authors/daniel-keys-moran-author|text=Daniel Keys Moran (Author)|author=[[Dennis Pellegrom]]|work=[[Star Wars Interviews]]|archivedate=20181007223111|nolive=1}}</ref> Trank and a teaser of the film, originally meant for a 2018 release, were scheduled to be featured at [[Celebration Anaheim (2015)|Celebration Anaheim]] on [[April 19]], [[2015]], but the convention appearance was cancelled and instead reorganized to focus only on director [[Gareth Edwards]] and ''Rogue One''. Lucasfilm was reportedly intending to announce the Fett-focused film during the panel, complete with a teaser reel.<ref name="Spinoffs EW">{{WebCite|url=https://ew.com/article/2016/11/22/rogue-one-lucasfilm-new-star-wars-movies|text=As ''Rogue One'' looms, Lucasfilm develops secret plans for new ''Star Wars'' movies|author=Anthony Breznican|date=2016-11-22|work=[[Entertainment Weekly Books|''Entertainment Weekly'']]|archivedate=20200107235510}}</ref> Shortly thereafter, on [[May 1]], 2015, it was announced that Trank had left the project.<ref name="Trank's Departure" />

According to Trank, his involvement with the project ended because he wanted to pursue smaller feature films.<ref name="Josh Trank Smaller Films">{{WebCite|url=https://www.latimes.com/entertainment/herocomplex/la-et-hc-josh-trank-on-why-he-dropped-out-of-star-wars-20150603-story.html|text=Josh Trank sets the story straight on why he left 'Star Wars'|author=Josh Rottenberg|date=2015-06-04|work=[[Wikipedia:Los Angeles Times|''Los Angeles Times'']]|archivedate=20200507175940}}</ref> Media outlets have reported, however, that the decision was made by Lucasfilm due to the troubled production on Trank's critically panned [[Wikipedia:Fantastic Four (2015 film)|''Fantastic Four'']] film.<ref name="THR Firing">{{WebCite|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/inside-a-star-wars-firing-792933|text=Inside a 'Star Wars' Firing: 'Fantastic Four' Problems Led to Director Josh Trank's Ouster|author=Kim Masters, Borys Kit=|work=[[Wikipedia:The Hollywood Reporter|''The Hollywood Reporter'']]|archivedate=20200511111700}}</ref> Trank had been vocal about wanting to pursue his own vision for ''Fantastic Four'' and claimed that studio meddling had gotten in the way of that. Although Trank attempted to minimize reports of issues between him and the studio, Lucasfilm president [[Kathleen Kennedy]] reportedly did not believe the spin, and he was let go from his ''Star Wars'' film.<ref name="EW Aug-2015" /> At the time, Kennedy told ''Entertainment Weekly'': "It's still one of the stories that we absolutely want to tell. There is a lot of innovative technology in and around what it is we're doing with that story, so for a lot of reasons, we were comfortable postponing that. But we're definitely still developing it."<ref name="EW Solo">{{WebCite|url=http://www.ew.com/article/2015/08/13/star-wars-anthology-young-han-solo/3|text=''Star Wars'' anthology: How young will Han Solo be?|author=Anthony Breznican|date=2015-08-13|work=[[Entertainment Weekly Books|''Entertainment Weekly'']]|archivedate=20151231061212}}</ref>

The 2018 release originally meant for the film was ultimately given to Kasdan's [[Han Solo]] picture,<ref name="Han Solo" /> directed by [[Ron Howard]].<ref name="Howard Director">{{SW|url=news/ron-howard-to-assume-directorial-duties-on-the-untitled-han-solo-film|text=Ron Howard to Assume Directorial Duties on the Untitled Han Solo Film}}</ref> Disney later confirmed that a third stand-alone film would be released in [[2020]], though neither the Boba Fett focus nor Kinberg's continued involvement were confirmed.<ref name="Date Confirmation">{{WebCite|url=https://www.theguardian.com/film/2016/sep/22/star-wars-anthology-movie-2020-disney-confirms-rogue-one|text=Disney confirms Star Wars anthology movie for 2020|author=Andrew Pulver|date=2016-09-22|work=[[Wikipedia:The Guardian|''The Guardian'']]|quote=Disney chief executive Bob Iger has confirmed that another entry in the Star Wars series of stand-alone "anthology" movies is set for release in 2020.|archivedate=20190419065625}}</ref> Kinberg, nonetheless, continued with Lucasfilm as an executive producer on the television series ''[[Star Wars Rebels]]''<ref name="Zero Hour">{{Rebels|Zero Hour}}</ref> and made his [[Wikipedia:List of directorial debuts|directorial debut]] with [[20th Century Studios|20th Century Fox's]] [[Wikipedia:Dark Phoenix (film)|''Dark Phoenix'']] with a [[February 14]], [[2019]], release.<ref name="Kinberg X-Men">{{WebCite|url=https://deadline.com/2017/06/x-men-dark-phoenix-jennifer-lawrence-michael-fassbender-james-mcavoy-simon-kinberg-1202113290|text=Fox Formalizes Simon Kinberg To Helm 'X-Men: Dark Phoenix'; Jennifer Lawrence, Michael Fassbender, James McAvoy Back, Jessica Chastain In Talks|author=Mike Fleming Jr|date=2017-06-14|work=[[Wikipedia:Deadline Hollywood|''Deadline'']]|archivedate=20191228194042}}</ref>

On [[August 17]], [[2017]], it was reported by ''The Hollywood Reporter'' that an [[Obi-Wan Kenobi]] spin-off film was in the works, with [[Stephen Daldry]] in early talks to direct. Furthermore, it was mentioned that films about Boba Fett and [[Yoda]], respectively, were still being considered for the future.<ref name="Obi-Wan HR">{{WebCite|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/obi-wan-kenobi-star-wars-film-planned-director-talks-1030505|text='Star Wars' Obi-Wan Kenobi Film in the Works (Exclusive)|author=Borys Kit|date=2017-08-17|work=[[Wikipedia:The Hollywood Reporter|''The Hollywood Reporter'']]|archivedate=20200505213215}}</ref><ref name="Obi-Wan Variety">{{WebCite|url=https://variety.com/2017/film/news/obi-wan-kenobi-star-wars-movie-disney-stephen-daldry-1202531346|text='Star Wars' Obi-Wan Kenobi Movie in Early Development at Disney|author=Justin Kroll|date=2017-08-17|work=[[Wikipedia:Variety (magazine)|''Variety'']]|archivedate=20200308132134}}</ref> ''The Hollywood Reporter'' reiterated its report about a Kenobi film on [[May 24]], [[2018]] while also stating that [[James Mangold]], director of [[Wikipedia:The Wolverine (film)|''The Wolverine'']] and [[Wikipedia:Logan (2017 film)|''Logan'']], would write and direct a Boba Fett film.<ref name="Boba Fett THR">{{WebCite|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/star-wars-boba-fett-movie-is-happening-james-mangold-direct-1113273|text='Star Wars': Boba Fett Movie in the Works With James Mangold (Exclusive)|author=Borys Kit|date=2018-05-24|work=[[Wikipedia:The Hollywood Reporter|''The Hollywood Reporter'']]|archivedate=20200510161002}}</ref> However, in [[September]] 2018, Bob Iger said that there would be a "slowdown" on the release of Star Wars films, because he felt there had been "a little too much, too fast."<ref>{{WebCite|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/bob-iger-disneys-streaming-service-james-gunn-star-wars-slowdown-1145493|text=Bob Iger Talks Disney's Streaming Service, 'Roseanne,' James Gunn and a Coming 'Star Wars' "Slowdown"|author=Matthew Belloni|date=2018-09-20|work=[[Wikipedia:The Hollywood Reporter|''The Hollywood Reporter'']]|archivedate=20200511001454}}</ref> He reaffirmed this in April 2019, saying "We will take a pause, some time, and reset &hellip; There will be other Stars Wars movies, but there will be a bit of a hiatus."<ref>{{WebCite|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2019-04-12/star-wars-movies-will-take-a-break-after-episode-ix-disney-says|text=Star Wars Movies Will Take a Break After Episode IX, Disney Says|date=2019-04-11|work=[[Wikipedia:Bloomberg News|''Bloomberg'']]|archivedate=20200404143815}}</ref>

In [[October]] 2018, it was reported that the planned film had been scrapped, with Lucasfilm instead focusing on the [[Jon Favreau]]-headed television series ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]''.<ref name="boba no">{{WebCite|url=https://www.cinemablend.com/news/2460214/the-boba-fett-movie-isnt-happening-anymore|text=The Boba Fett Movie Isn't Happening Anymore|author=Adam Holmes|date=2018-10-26|work=[https://www.cinemablend.com CinemaBlend]|archivedate=20190425071803}}</ref> On [[February 22]], [[2019]], it was reported by [[Wikipedia:CNBC|CNBC]] that [[Hasbro]] will see a dip in sales in 2020 with the absence of a new feature, indicating that the film has been removed from its initial schedule.<ref name="hasbro">{{WebCite|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2019/02/22/no-star-wars-film-in-2020-but-there-will-be-plenty-of-toys.html|text=No 'Star Wars' film is slated for 2020, but there will still be plenty of toys|author=Sarah Whitten|date=2019-02-22|work=[[Wikipedia:CNBC|CNBC]]|archivedate=20190226205335}}</ref> On [[May 7]], 2019, Disney released its 2020-2027 theatrical release schedule, which revealed that ''Star Wars'' films are scheduled to hit theaters in [[2023]], [[2025]], and [[2027]], but not 2020.<ref name="Variety">{{WebCite|url=https://variety.com/2019/film/news/disney-avatar-star-wars-marvel-1203207661|text=Disney Moves 'Avatar' Sequels, Announces New 'Star Wars' Films|author=Brent Lang|date=2019-05-07|work=[[Wikipedia=Variety (magazine)|''Variety'']]|archivedate=20200505213259}}</ref><ref name="Variety Disney list">{{WebCite|url=https://variety.com/2020/film/box-office/star-wars-films-avatar-sequels-pushed-back-a-year-in-disney-release-calendar-shakeup-1234715104|text='Star Wars' Films, 'Avatar' Sequels Pushed Back a Year in Disney Release Calendar Shakeup|author=Brent Lang|date=2020-07-23|work=[[Wikipedia=Variety (magazine)|''Variety'']]|archiveurl=https://archive.is/YZUqD}}</ref> On [[August 30]], [[2023]], Kinberg confirmed that he was going to write the Boba Fett film,<ref name="Polygon" /> which was ultimately replaced by ''[[Star Wars: The Book of Boba Fett]]'', a series set in the time of ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]''.<ref name="BOBF Announcement">{{SW|url=news/the-book-of-boba-fett|text=''The Book of Boba Fett'', a New Series, Coming December 2021 on Disney+}}</ref>

